/*
  /lab/music — a LOCAL-ONLY bench, dev-gated below, linked from nowhere.

  THE QUESTION, narrowed 2026-08-10: **the palette is the room — now does it
  hold up with the music actually in it?** Michael, after driving A/B/C:
  "Option B is a clear winner here. Can we orient the lab around that only?"

  A (the /blog rail, re-pointed) and C (songs shelved by where their arc landed)
  were deleted the same day rather than kept as controls. The rule the 2026-08-10
  sweep set is that a bench is legitimate while its question is live, and "which
  of three shapes" stopped being live the moment it was answered. What A was —
  stacking AND filters with contextual counts and dead ends disabled — survives
  underneath this page, because B was never a different query, only a different
  act. See `git log -- src/pages/lab/music.astro` for the losers.

  ⚠ DIRECTION IS GONE, and this is a claim about jazz rather than about UI.
  C rendered a song as an ordered arc (`regretful → redemptive`) on the theory
  that a sequence is an object. Michael killed it on sight: "many songs go back
  and forth (since most jazz songs have heads anyway)". A head-solos-head form
  RETURNS to where it started, so an ordered list of feelings describes a shape
  the music does not have — and worse, it would cost real authoring time to
  record something false. A song's feelings are a SET. What looked like the most
  distinctive idea here was the first thing the material refused.

  ⚠ THE WORDS ARE SIZED BY COUNT, which overrules the argument this file made
  yesterday. The first draft kept the field EVEN at rest, reasoning that sizing
  by frequency "would make the library's accidents look like the vocabulary's
  shape". Michael, 2026-08-10: "can we make the words different sizes based on
  how many songs match? and better yet, make it dynamic as the filters change?"
  He is right, and the earlier argument had the cost backwards: an even field
  hides that some feelings are rare, and rarity is the most interesting thing a
  spectrum can tell you about the person who built it. The field now collapses
  visibly toward what is still reachable as you choose.

  ⚠ "PAIRED WITH", NOT "GOES WITH" — Michael's wording, 2026-08-10. It is the
  word the schema and both ADRs already use (`paired_song_id`, ADR-0011's title),
  so the room now says the same thing the rest of the site says. "Goes with"
  was a description of the relation; "paired with" is its name.

  ⚠ EVERY FEELING BELOW IS INVENTED. Titles, artists and Spotify/YouTube URLs
  are real — 48 rows from the live corpus, so the players actually play and long
  jazz titles stress the layout for real. THE FEELINGS ATTACHED TO THEM ARE
  MINE, NOT MICHAEL'S. Nothing here is a reading of a song. That is also why the
  PROD gate on the first line of the body is not optional: /reading shipped a
  fabricated essay one route from the real one, un-gated and indexable, and no
  search engine could tell which was the writing.

  ⚠ NO DATABASE WORK WAS DONE FOR THIS. No migration, no tables, no schema
  opinion. The fixture is hardcoded and the whole bench is one deletable file.

  ⚠ THE ROOM OPENS FULL, NOT EMPTY. It showed nothing until you chose a word,
  until Michael's call on 2026-08-10: "I still want the user to be able to
  browse the music that I like, because we still show the words beneath each of
  the songs. That still gives them affordance to browse based off of those
  feelings." That is the show-don't-tell argument doing real work — an empty
  room has to EXPLAIN itself, whereas a full one demonstrates the mechanism,
  because every song is already wearing the words you would filter by. Browsing
  and filtering stopped being two states; no selection simply means no
  narrowing, and `holds()` returns true for an empty word list.

  ⚠ A PLAYER LIVES EXACTLY AS LONG AS ITS SONG IS IN THE ROOM. It mounts when
  the card comes within 600px of the viewport, survives every filter change that
  keeps its song in the set, and is destroyed when the song leaves. Say the rule
  that way and the two halves stop looking contradictory: what "never destroy"
  was ever protecting is that NARROWING must not kill music still on screen. A
  song the filter excluded is not on screen, so its sound has no business
  outliving it. Removing the iframe is also the only pause we have — a
  cross-origin frame cannot be controlled from outside.

  ⚠ AND THIS REPLACED A STICKY DOCK, deleted 2026-08-10 the same day it was
  built. Michael: "if the user starts playing a song and then they lose track of
  where it was, they can't actually stop the song." True, and the first answer
  was to pin the playing card to the corner with `position: fixed` (never a DOM
  move — re-parenting an <iframe> reloads it, which would stop the music the
  dock existed to stop). The mechanism worked; the TRIGGER could not. Nothing
  cross-origin tells us a frame is playing, so it docked on focus — window blur
  plus `document.activeElement` — and in a real browser that fired on the wrong
  frame and then never fired again. It also docked players you had paused,
  because those are indistinguishable from here.

  The honest version needs Spotify's iFrame API (`createController` →
  `playback_update`) for real transport state, plus a second integration for
  YouTube — a dependency and a failure surface, bought for a room whose
  vocabulary is undecided and whose corpus is untagged. Michael, correctly:
  "do we do the sensible thing and stop embeds from playing if it disappears
  from the filters?" That is what the rule above does, and it dissolves three
  problems at once — you can never lose a player, frames stop accumulating, and
  fewer live iframes means less to composite. Revisit the API only if a shipped
  room proves the problem is real. The prototype is in
  `git log -- src/pages/lab/music.astro`.

  WHAT TO JUDGE:
    1. The field at rest. Sizes are total matches, so this is the shape of the
       spectrum. Does the big/small distribution read as true, or as noise?
    2. Pick one word, then a second, then a third. Watch the field collapse.
       380ms is a guess and it is the number most worth arguing about — too
       slow and it feels sedated, too fast and the collapse is invisible.
       Measured after the FLIP/phasing fix (dev server, 1280×1000): zero long
       tasks per click, zero frames over 32ms, worst frame 16.8ms, and the
       field's LAYOUT settles into exactly ONE arrangement per change instead
       of re-wrapping every frame. If it still reads as janky, the cause is the
       curve or the duration, not the machinery.
    3. Start a track, then change the selection while it plays. The player must
       survive as long as its song stays in the set.
    4. A song's own words are filters. Click one from inside a result and judge
       whether "more like this" is the move you actually reach for.
    5. `corpus: 12` — the room at the size it will ACTUALLY open at.
    6. The rest state, which is the question uncontainerising asked and
       browsing answered. At full bleed the old empty room read as unfinished
       (composed at 1280 inside a border, abandoned without one); the fix was
       not vertical centring or a bigger type ramp but CONTENT. Judge whether
       the corpus under the field reads as an invitation or as a wall.
    7. Arrive and immediately scroll fast. Players mount 600px ahead, so the
       question is whether that margin is enough on a trackpad flick — too
       small and you scroll into empty boxes.
    8. Press play, then filter that song away. The sound stops with it — but
       measured, not instantly: the fade is 300ms, teardown starts after the
       380ms collapse so it cannot collide with it, and the last frame is
       released about 470ms after that. Call it half a second to a second of
       tail. That delay is the price of the smooth collapse, and it is the
       right way round only if the tail reads as natural rather than as a bug.
       Judge that. It is also abrupt at the end by necessity: volume is not
       ours to fade.
    9. THE TWO SHAPES OF PLAYER, which only became a problem once the room
       opened full. A Spotify track is a 152px row; a YouTube video is a 16:9
       block roughly twice as tall, and three of the 48 are YouTube. In fixture
       order two of them land first, so the room currently opens on two big
       video frames and the eye reads them as the feature rather than as two
       rows that happen to be taller. Not reordered here, because the fix is a
       real decision and not a fixture tweak: either video gets normalised to
       the row height everything else uses, or the room needs an order that
       isn't "whatever the corpus hands back".

  ⚠ NOTE ON MEASUREMENT: every number in this file is from the DEV SERVER.
  The PROD gate on the first line of the body means this page redirects in a
  production build, so it cannot be profiled there — which is correct for the
  gate and a real limit on the numbers. Treat them as upper bounds.

  ⚠ FRAMES NO LONGER ACCUMULATE, which was the bill the old never-destroy rule
  ran up: browsing and clearing used to leave 33 hidden players mounted. Now the
  live set is bounded by what the current filter shows and what you have scrolled
  past, and narrowing actively gives frames back.
  (Spotify's own frame logs `compute-pressure is not allowed` — its request, not
  ours, and not worth widening `allow` in src/lib/media.ts for.)

  WHAT THIS BENCH STILL CANNOT ANSWER:
    • Whether the vocabulary is the right sixteen. Discovered by tagging, not
      by drafting.
    • Whether adding-and-tagging a song is affordable. That is the next bench
      and it is the one that decides this — and per plan 33's ruling 1 it is
      ONE act, not two: there is no bulk import and no AI pass, because a
      feeling is not a property of the song, it is what happened in Michael.
      The corpus grows one song at a time, as a byproduct of listening.
*/
